00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:40.348
So this is pretty cool.

00:01:40.348 --> 00:01:43.290
This is introducing
container-native load balancing

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.540
on Google Kubernetes engine.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.779
So this is quite neat in
that previously you've

00:01:47.779 --> 00:01:49.695
had things like Ingress,
you've had Services--

00:01:49.695 --> 00:01:51.090
that's all been really great.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.100
And generally speaking,
that's been fine.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.920
Usually when that works, it
comes down through the nose,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.890
and through iptables, and
then directly into the pods,

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.786
and sort of routed
through Kubernetes itself.

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:02.910
This cloud native load
balancing works a little bit

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:06.090
more efficiently in that it
comes straight into a network

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.730
endpoint group and actually gets
passed directly into the pods

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:09.230
themselves.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:12.420
So it basically provides optimal
load balancing, native support

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.820
for health checking, some
graceful termination,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.400
optimal data parts, increased
visibility and security.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.945
So it's quite a
neat little project.

00:02:18.945 --> 00:02:20.380
You should probably
check it out.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.894
It is the network
endpoint groups,

00:02:21.894 --> 00:02:24.060
so make sure and check it
out, and we'll have links.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:24.810
MELANIE WARRICK:
