00:07:06.574 --> 00:07:08.490
MELANIE WARRICK: For
those who might not know,

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:09.420
can you tell us
a little bit more

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.160
about what are
Spring Frameworks?

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.880
MIKE ELTSUFIN: Sure,
so Spring Framework

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.840
has been around for a long
time, well over 10 years now.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.200
It started as a dependency
injection framework for Java.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.610
And it was also sort of
an alternative to the JEE

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.140
framework that a
lot of people were

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.480
using to build
enterprise applications.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.210
It has grown quite
a bit since then

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.670
and has become more of
an umbrella framework

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:36.510
with all kinds of integrations
for enterprise application

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.140
development.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.690
And in the past few
years, they also

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:43.470
brought Spring Boot
which made it even easier

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.710
to build enterprise
applications by taking advantage

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.840
of this kind of principle of
convention over configuration.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.170
So that that's Spring
and Spring Boot.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:55.660
They're fairly popular
today among Java developers,

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:58.550
especially the
enterprise applications.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:00.510
And that's part of
the reason why we

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:01.991
are focusing on that framework.

00:08:01.991 --> 00:08:03.740
MARK MANDEL: What's
our integration there?

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.720
What are we actually doing to
make like Spring and Spring

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.861
Boot easier for
Google Cloud Platform?

00:08:08.861 --> 00:08:11.360
Because, I mean, Spring's an
inversion of control framework,

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.690
and Spring Boot's and
MVC-type framework.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:14.847
So what's the deal there?

00:08:14.847 --> 00:08:17.430
MIKE ELTSUFIN: So there's a lot
of things in Spring Framework,

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.240
Spring Boot--

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.330
a lot of those things are
integrations with existing

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.820
tools that are useful for
enterprise application

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.710
development, like messaging
systems, different databases.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:29.610
They really try to make
it very easy to use those.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.159
However, there are no
out-of-the-box integrations,

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.340
at least in the past, that
would work with Google Cloud.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.230
So what we've done
is we've basically

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.080
looked at all the different
sub-projects within Spring

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.119
Framework and try to find
a match in the Google Cloud

00:08:44.119 --> 00:08:47.850
APIs and services where we
could apply those abstractions

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:51.390
and idioms and implement
those bindings, essentially.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.730
So we've done that for a
bunch of different services

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:57.600
in Google Cloud now, including
databases like Spanner,

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:02.250
Datastore, CloudSQL, as well
as messaging, so Pub/Sub,

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.920
[INAUDIBLE], configuration.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.470
Almost anywhere where
there is some kind of match

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:09.810
between Spring Framework
and Google Cloud,

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:13.892
we try to make that work
better through Spring.

00:09:13.892 --> 00:09:15.600
MARK MANDEL: So does
the Spring Framework

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.677
have some kind of
higher-level abstraction?

00:09:17.677 --> 00:09:19.260
Is that how the
developer sees things?

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:20.160
Is that how that works?

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.950
MIKE ELTSUFIN: Yeah, they have
a whole bunch of abstractions

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:23.410
for different purposes.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:24.990
For example, for
databases they have

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:26.670
a project called Spring Data.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.500
There are some interfaces
and abstractions

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.530
that apply across different
Spring Data implementations,

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:35.110
but they also leave the
flexibility to kind of expose

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:38.910
the specific features of a
database through that as well.

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:41.950
So as much as Spanner's
a relational database,

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.360
but there are some very
unique features of Spanner,

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:46.650
and we're able to expose
the unique features

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:50.370
as well as kind of comply
with idiomatic Spring Data

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:52.320
interfaces so that
people can easily

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:54.089
migrate from one
database to another,

00:09:54.089 --> 00:09:56.130
yet they're still able to
use the unique features

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:57.210
if they need to.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.290
MELANIE WARRICK: Why
is Google investing?

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.110
Why does this matter
for Google to develop

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:02.896
Spring Cloud for GCP?

00:10:02.896 --> 00:10:04.770
MIKE ELTSUFIN: We care
about Java developers.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.304
We want them to be
happy on GCP, so--

00:10:07.304 --> 00:10:08.470
MELANIE WARRICK: Do we care?

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:09.360
Do we really care?

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:10.890
No, we care.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:11.700
MIKE ELTSUFIN: We
really want to make

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.240
that experience great for them.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:15.660
And we know that a
lot of Java developers

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.390
are on Spring
Framework, and we really

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:19.860
want to make it as
easy as possible

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:22.290
for them to move their
applications to GCP

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:25.260
or write new applications
using Spring Framework for GCP.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:26.760
And we know that
there are a bunch

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.090
of benefits to using Spring
Framework and Spring Boot.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.607
for example, it eliminates a
lot of the boilerplate code

00:10:32.607 --> 00:10:34.440
that you have to write,
so it's a lot easier

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:35.641
to get started with.

00:10:35.641 --> 00:10:37.890
We're really trying to meet
developers where they are,

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:39.360
and where they are.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.850
is using Spring Framework,
as far as we can tell.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:42.780
MELANIE WARRICK:
That makes sense.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.550
MARK MANDEL: From a
developer perspective,

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:45.540
how do I set this up?

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.620
If I have a GCP project, do
I need to import credentials,

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:50.700
or is it seamless?

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:51.630
How does this work?

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:53.880
MIKE ELTSUFIN: Yeah, so we
have a bunch of Spring Boot

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:54.510
starters.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:57.210
So I mentioned convention
over configuration--

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:00.470
so we tried to not make you
configure too many things

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.360
and instead auto-discover
whenever possible,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:06.030
yet allowing, also, to
override those defaults.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.070
With credentials,
for example, as soon

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:12.240
as you bring in our core
starter from Spring Cloud GCP,

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.060
it will try to
auto-detect credentials

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:15.060
wherever you're running.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.476
If you're running
locally, it will

00:11:16.476 --> 00:11:19.260
try to find your local
credentials that you've set up

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:23.590
using environment variables or G
Cloud If you're running on GCP,

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:26.680
it will also try to
auto-discover the normal places

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:31.060
where you'd find the credentials
on a GCE or what have you.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:34.770
Alternatively, you could always
specify a path to the key,

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.140
so we'll add that to be
a possibility as well.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:39.510
And for a lot of these
things, we kind of

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:41.610
make assumptions for
the configuration,

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.350
and you can always
override that.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:44.130
MARK MANDEL: I guess
that's a nice, then.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:46.290
It means if I'm using
Spanner or Google Cloud

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.047
Storage as a
development thing, I

00:11:48.047 --> 00:11:49.380
don't have to have that locally.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:50.580
And my local
environment can just

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:52.470
talk straight up to whatever
my development environment is.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:53.730
MIKE ELTSUFIN: If you're
developing locally

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:55.438
and you want to use
Spanner, of course it

00:11:55.438 --> 00:11:57.330
will need to talk
to Spanner remotely,

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:59.125
because there is no
emulator for a Spanner.

00:11:59.125 --> 00:11:59.530
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.230
MIKE ELTSUFIN: If you
already have G Cloud set up,

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.407
and you set up credentials,
it will automatically

00:12:03.407 --> 00:12:04.740
use those to connect to spanner.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:06.260
You don't need to really
configure anything.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.970
You just add our starter, and
it will work out of the box.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:09.990
MELANIE WARRICK: I
was going to ask,

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:11.573
what are some of the
challenges you've

00:12:11.573 --> 00:12:13.860
seen that you're working
on trying to address

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:15.212
for some of the users now?

00:12:15.212 --> 00:12:16.920
MIKE ELTSUFIN: At
first, when we started,

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.230
we were trying to
basically guess

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:21.375
what the developers will
need, like which parts

00:12:21.375 --> 00:12:23.580
to expose to the
Spring abstractions

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:24.997
and which ones we should hide.

00:12:24.997 --> 00:12:26.580
It's challenging
when you don't really

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:29.550
have too many users
giving feedback initially.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:32.280
But now that we've kind of
gone through the first official

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:34.170
release, we're getting
a lot more feedback,

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:36.930
and we're getting users
engaged on GitHub.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:40.710
So it's getting a bit better
to set direction on what

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:41.870
we should be focusing on.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.745
And there are so many
different possibilities

00:12:43.745 --> 00:12:46.530
where we could do integration
between Spring and GCP.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:49.650
And just prioritizing those
things are sometimes difficult.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:51.809
So getting the user
feedback is very important.

00:12:51.809 --> 00:12:53.100
MARK MANDEL: That sounds great.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:54.724
MIKE ELTSUFIN: This
project is actually

00:12:54.724 --> 00:12:57.060
a close collaboration
with the Spring team,

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:58.680
the creators of the
Spring Framework,

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.200
and Pivotal, which is the
company that houses them.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:03.690
We've been working with
them from the very beginning

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:04.530
on this.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:07.620
And they've been helping set
direction on this project,

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.510
and they've been
actively guiding us

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:11.340
throughout the whole process.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.910
We frequently meet with
them and talk about design,

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.600
implementation, all of that.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:18.710
So it's a very close
partnership with Pivotal

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:19.840
and Spring team on this.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.890
So we have that expertise being
funneled into the project,

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:25.892
as well as from our side,
the Google Cloud expertise.

00:13:25.892 --> 00:13:26.850
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.014
So if I'm running a
Spring Boot application,

00:13:29.014 --> 00:13:30.930
and I'm not that familiar
with that ecosystem,

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.660
are there any restrictions
on where I can run that app?

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:36.754
Is it just on App Engine maybe,
or just in a Docker container?

00:13:36.754 --> 00:13:38.170
MIKE ELTSUFIN:
Yeah, at this point

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:39.782
you can pretty much
run it anywhere.

00:13:39.782 --> 00:13:40.740
It works on App Engine.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:42.130
It works on Kubernetes.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:45.480
It works on GCE, wherever
you can run Java.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.790
Spring Boot is essentially
an executable JAR,

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:51.760
so whenever you can run that,
it can also run as a WAR file.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:53.940
So if you're using
App Engine standard,

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:55.159
you could also do it there.

00:13:55.159 --> 00:13:56.700
MARK MANDEL: So
speaking of runtimes,

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.790
we have some new tools for being
able to run Java applications

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:00.630
inside containers.

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:01.800
Patrick, I know you're
joining us today

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:02.710
to talk to us about that.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:04.350
Do you want to give us
a little rundown on Jib?

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:05.891
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah,
I'd be happy to.

00:14:05.891 --> 00:14:07.480
So what is Jib?

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:11.580
Jib is basically
an answer to what

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:15.600
should be the right way
to containerize a Java

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:16.530
application.

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:18.660
The thing that
kind of stands out

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.510
about using Jib from the way
that people traditionally

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:23.241
containerize using
Docker files--

00:14:23.241 --> 00:14:24.490
there are a few things really.

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:27.330
The first is that you don't
need a Docker daemon running

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:29.490
on your machine,
which requires root.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:32.520
And that kind of suits a lot
of people as well as enterprise

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.530
CI environments, which
have problems with that.

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:36.900
That wasn't the focus
for why we built Jib,

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.150
but it was kind of a nice
surprise that a lot of users

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:40.530
adopted it for that reason.

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:43.590
The main reasons we focused
on, on building Jib,

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:45.750
is because we felt that
the way that people

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.210
were packaging containers
using Docker file

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:49.690
tended to be inefficient.

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:52.350
So Jib typically will
build the Docker container

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.630
kind of marginally faster
in your first build.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.240
But then the incremental builds
can be dramatically faster,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:58.450
depending on the project.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.320
And then the other aspect of
Jib that's pretty important

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.630
is that it's much
more declarative.

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:08.010
While a Docker file will allow
you to run arbitrary commands

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.970
and, therefore, it's pretty
much not a reproducible build,

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:13.560
a Jib build config
is declarative.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:17.160
And that makes it really
easy for end users.

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:19.380
It means that we can
set a lot of defaults,

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:20.970
which means that you
have to configure

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:22.620
very little to get started.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.720
And it allows our
tooling to sort

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:27.180
of incrementally
optimize both the build

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:28.680
and the eventual runtime.

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.270
So yeah, it's just
it just makes it

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.000
a lot easier for
Java developers who

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.740
have this hump of
needing to turn

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.500
their traditional packaging
formats into containers

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.934
so they can target new
platforms like Kubernetes.

00:15:39.934 --> 00:15:41.850
MARK MANDEL: And from a
developer perspective,

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:42.900
how do I use jib?

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.400
PATRICK FLYNN:
Right, good question.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:46.470
First, I'll start with
a little bit of context.

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:50.610
Jib actually came from this
local container guru at Google

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:52.620
called Matthew Moore.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:54.510
If you're not
familiar with Basel,

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.574
Bazel is this
Google build system,

00:15:56.574 --> 00:15:57.990
which we use
internally, and which

00:15:57.990 --> 00:15:59.550
we've open sourced recently.

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:02.640
He built a bunch of
containerizers for Bazel

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:04.430
that worked very much like Jib.

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:05.940
But what we've
seen is that Bazel

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:08.940
is like a big hump for
people to adopt a better

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:10.840
way to containerize
their applications.

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:13.280
Most of our Java users
are on Maven or Gradle.

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.960
So what Jib does is it
takes a lot of that strategy

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:20.220
that the Bazel rules use and
implements it as a Java library

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.750
and integrates it into
Maven and Gradle plugins.

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:24.780
So if you wanted to use
Jib, all you have to do

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:27.960
is just set up your pom.xml,
or your build.gradle

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:29.280
to use our plugin.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:31.590
And the most minimal
config you really need

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.740
is just specifying where
your container registry

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:35.820
images should be pushed.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.010
MELANIE WARRICK: You'd mentioned
about Jib being more efficient

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:38.550
than Docker.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:39.924
Can you explain
a little bit more

00:16:39.924 --> 00:16:41.280
about how it is more efficient?

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:42.780
PATRICK FLYNN: First
of all, I think

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:46.350
Docker file was a really
great solution initially

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:48.600
to bring people into
the world of containers.

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:51.180
It's very flexible,
and it gives people

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:54.300
the tools they need to take
whatever arbitrary packages

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:56.170
they have and containerize them.

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:59.190
That being said, you really need
to understand a lot of things

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:03.150
about how Docker works to
optimally package your apps

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:05.500
and have a good
incremental build time.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.260
So the first thing
that we do is we kind

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:09.990
of standardize the way
that we build our images.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:12.630
And we layer them
in a way that makes

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:14.250
more sense for incrementality.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:16.339
So for instance,
your dependencies--

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:18.569
so a lot of projects could
have tens or hundreds

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:21.170
of megabytes of JARs that
they use in their project.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:24.030
Your dependencies will be put
into a Docker layer that will

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:25.680
probably not change very often.

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:27.359
And your source code
and your resources

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:28.874
also go into separate layers.

00:17:28.874 --> 00:17:31.290
What that means is that, when
you do an incremental build,

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:34.080
instead of having to
repackage the entire container

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:36.840
and then push possibly
hundreds of megabytes,

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:38.604
you'll probably--
if your classes are

00:17:38.604 --> 00:17:40.020
only a few megabytes--
you'll only

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.540
have to push that
change to the registry

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.249
to actually get that
new image out there.

00:17:44.249 --> 00:17:46.206
MARK MANDEL: How does
this work without Docker?

00:17:46.206 --> 00:17:47.080
Like, is this magic?

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:47.940
MELANIE WARRICK:
Everything's magic.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.070
PATRICK FLYNN: I always assumed
that there is a lot of magic

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:50.570
there.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:52.950
But when you kind of dig into
the container image spec,

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:55.180
it's not that complicated.

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:58.230
It's really a bunch of
tarballs with metadata.

00:17:58.230 --> 00:17:59.910
So that's what the
layers are, kind

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.710
of these concentric tarballs.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:05.070
So we just built a library
that anybody can use actually

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:07.530
if they want to integrate it
into their system of choice

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:11.247
to help you build a container
image using [INAUDIBLE]..

00:18:11.247 --> 00:18:12.330
MARK MANDEL: This is nice.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:14.070
Does this fit
anywhere specifically

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:16.040
into a Kubernetes
workflow, or are there

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:17.130
any hooks in that way?

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:18.963
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah,
that's a good question.

00:18:18.963 --> 00:18:21.930
The funny thing is, if you
talk to Kubernetes developers,

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:25.050
there's not really much of a
standard around a workflow.

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:26.460
Most people are
kind of asking me

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:28.150
about what their
workflow should be,

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.960
which is a shame, because I want
to find out what there's is.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:35.190
And the one tool that we've
seen gain significant adoption

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:39.390
has been Scaffold, which
is a CLI tool also provided

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:43.380
by Google that basically will
wash your microservice project

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:46.590
or a set of microservice
projects based off of a YAML,

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:48.720
because this is Kubernetes,
and will actually

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:51.330
trigger rebuilds, and
push those images,

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:55.260
and update your cluster
config so that you kind of get

00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:57.990
a hot reload feel
from your application,

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.420
assuming that your container
builds are fast enough.

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:02.070
So what we've been
doing is trying

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:05.460
to make sure that scaffold
users who are Java users

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:06.720
have a decent experience.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:09.060
Scaffold in Java is not
great at the moment,

00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:12.270
because as you can imagine,
a file watcher that basically

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:14.730
rebuilds on every
file change for Java

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:17.010
doesn't necessarily
make sense, especially

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:19.690
given like how heavyweight
some Java builds can be.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:20.989
So we've been looking at that.

00:19:20.989 --> 00:19:23.280
Some users have had, actually,
some pretty good success

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:26.730
by using this task that we
have in both of our plugins,

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:28.770
which generates
a Docker context.

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:31.760
So you get, effectively, kind
of the Docker version of what

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:34.230
Jib would have created and
pointing Scaffold at that

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:36.270
because Scaffold
understands Docker.

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:38.130
But we're actually
working currently

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:42.000
with the Scaffold team to build
a Jib builder for Scaffold.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:42.750
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.820
PATRICK FLYNN: And that should
be available within the month

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:45.510
or so.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:47.718
MELANIE WARRICK: What do
you enjoy most about working

00:19:47.718 --> 00:19:48.900
with Jib and with Java?

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:50.340
PATRICK FLYNN: What I like
about working with Jib

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:52.080
is that I don't
have to think too

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:53.550
much about containerization.

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.970
And what I think is
powerful also about the way

00:19:56.970 --> 00:19:58.680
that Jib packages your app--

00:19:58.680 --> 00:20:02.850
because Jib doesn't take your
JAR or uber-JAR, or your WAR

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:05.340
and put it into a
Docker container.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:06.971
It understands Java.

00:20:06.971 --> 00:20:08.970
It understands that you
have resources, classes,

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:10.110
and libraries.

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:12.440
And it lays out a file system.

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:14.460
And the real package
is the container.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.150
I think what people have sort
of missed about containerization

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:19.730
is that they've started like
packaging their packages.

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:23.450
And a lot of the complexity that
you see in the Java ecosystem

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:27.260
around uber-JARs
or executable JARs,

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:30.110
they're not really relevant
in the container world, where

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.480
you have your entry point,
and you can just arbitrarily

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:34.040
lay out files any
way that you like

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:36.080
and make it work, because you
have control over the Java

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:36.680
invocation.

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:38.930
And so that's what
kind of excites me

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:42.710
about Jib and the way it makes
containers a native packaging

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:43.550
format for Java.

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:44.180
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:45.554
PATRICK FLYNN:
The Java ecosystem

00:20:45.554 --> 00:20:48.080
itself, I just think it's
amazing how long it's lasted

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:49.070
and how huge it is.

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:51.320
And it's incredibly
important for Google.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:55.820
I think Java is the largest
code base internally at Google,

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:58.310
and we work with the
internal Java team as well.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:00.530
A lot of the inspiration
for the way that Jib works

00:21:00.530 --> 00:21:03.530
comes from the way that Google
containerizes applications

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:04.970
and has been for many years now.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:06.952
I just love that
community out there, too.

00:21:06.952 --> 00:21:08.660
I don't know if you've
heard of JHipster,

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:10.380
and Spring, and Micronaut.

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.140
There's just a lot of
innovation actually

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:15.602
happening in that ecosystem, as
well as the new JVM languages

00:21:15.602 --> 00:21:17.810
that are getting popular,
like Kotlin, in particular,

00:21:17.810 --> 00:21:20.000
with the adoption
it's had on Android.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:21.420
It's just really exciting.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:22.340
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
wondering if I'm

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:23.923
going to date myself
for how long I've

00:21:23.923 --> 00:21:25.340
been out of the Java ecosystem.

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:27.620
Let's say I'm building a web
server with something like Jib.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.286
I assume I still need
to embed something

00:21:29.286 --> 00:21:31.940
like a Jetty or something
that actually runs the server.

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:32.810
How do I tell it--

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:34.140
how do I expose ports?

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.039
Is there a best practices there?

00:21:36.039 --> 00:21:36.830
PATRICK FLYNN: Yes.

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:38.810
Currently the type
of applications

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:42.680
that we support in Jib are
only the Java dash type

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:43.370
applications.

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:44.874
They're not WAR
style applications,

00:21:44.874 --> 00:21:46.290
although we're
adding WAR support,

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:47.998
and that should be
available pretty soon.

00:21:47.998 --> 00:21:50.810
So anything that you could run
on your machine using Java dash

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:52.940
CP will work in Jib.

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:55.610
And what people typically
do for web services,

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:58.417
either they use Spring Boot
or some analog of that.

00:21:58.417 --> 00:21:59.750
MARK MANDEL: I'm showing my age.

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:01.666
PATRICK FLYNN: Micronaut
is another framework.

00:22:01.666 --> 00:22:02.810
There's a Play framework.

00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:03.440
MARK MANDEL: There's a bunch.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:04.310
PATRICK FLYNN: There's
so many of them.

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:06.601
MIKE ELTSUFIN: Yes, from the
Spring Boot point of view,

00:22:06.601 --> 00:22:10.790
it produces a JAR, but it really
embed either Tomcat, or Jetty,

00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:14.360
or more recently, it's actually
able to use a lower level

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.236
API which is called
Netty, a Netty server.

00:22:16.236 --> 00:22:17.360
It's much more lightweight.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:19.560
It's reactive, so
it's really cool.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.994
In the new version of Spring
you could use that as well.

00:22:21.994 --> 00:22:23.660
PATRICK FLYNN: And
since this is Google,

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:25.880
you can also, obviously,
create a gRPC endpoint--

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:26.480
MARK MANDEL: Of course.

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:28.310
PATRICK FLYNN: --very
easily, and package that.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:30.018
MARK MANDEL: And since
you brought it up,

00:22:30.018 --> 00:22:31.850
Mike, is there a
Jib, Spring Boot,

00:22:31.850 --> 00:22:35.680
Google Cloud mashup musical
thing happening at some point?

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:36.350
Is that a thing?

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:37.680
Is there a workflow that way?

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:38.620
MELANIE WARRICK:
Especially musical--

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:39.650
it needs to be musical.

00:22:39.650 --> 00:22:39.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:41.300
MARK MANDEL: It
needs to be musical.

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:42.080
MIKE ELTSUFIN: Yeah,
I'm probably not

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:43.740
the best person for
the musical part.

00:22:43.740 --> 00:22:44.240
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:45.560
But we can get help for that.

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:46.950
There's no reason
why I shouldn't work,

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:48.449
I'm pretty sure
people have done it.

00:22:48.449 --> 00:22:51.080
I don't know, Patrick, if you're
aware of any samples, demos,

00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:52.190
that we have for that?

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:54.481
PATRICK FLYNN: You know,
they're very orthogonal tools,

00:22:54.481 --> 00:22:56.160
so they just work well together.

00:22:56.160 --> 00:23:00.162
I think there was a demo
at SpringOne for Knative

00:23:00.162 --> 00:23:02.120
where you can actually--
one of the cool things

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:04.369
about both Maven and Gradle
is that you don't actually

00:23:04.369 --> 00:23:06.350
have to configure a
plugin internally in your

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:08.070
build file to actually use it.

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:11.030
So what a Knative does with
their build template sets,

00:23:11.030 --> 00:23:13.580
they have a Jib build template
that you can basically check

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:15.290
out any Spring Boot project.

00:23:15.290 --> 00:23:18.230
And it'll build a container
without modifying the project

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:18.990
at all.

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:21.350
So that's a really nice
and easy entry point

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:23.990
to just go from an arbitrary
Spring Boot project

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:25.797
to something that's
running in Kubernetes.

00:23:25.797 --> 00:23:27.755
MIKE ELTSUFIN: The other
thing with Spring Boot

00:23:27.755 --> 00:23:30.680
is you're really write very
little code in Spring Boot.

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:32.390
There's a lot of
other dependencies

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:33.410
you might bring in.

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:35.510
And while you're
developing the application,

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:38.180
the fact that you're only
modifying this one class file,

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:40.675
Jib makes it very efficient
to update your container,

00:23:40.675 --> 00:23:42.050
because it doesn't
have to update

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:43.424
all those other
dependencies that

00:23:43.424 --> 00:23:45.550
are not changing in the
process of the development.

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:47.132
MELANIE WARRICK: So
how can developers

00:23:47.132 --> 00:23:49.105
get involved in contributing
to these projects?

00:23:49.105 --> 00:23:51.480
MIKE ELTSUFIN: Spring Cloud
GCP is obviously open source.

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.180
You can find us on GitHub.

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:56.540
We love all kinds of
contributions, feedback,

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:59.392
issue submissions,
bugs, pull requests--

00:23:59.392 --> 00:24:00.350
all of that is welcome.

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:03.390
We always love when we see
the community contributing

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:04.890
and getting involved
in the project.

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:07.440
So just get on our
GitHub, and go from there.

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:08.720
PATRICK FLYNN: Yeah, I
would say the same for Jib.

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:11.101
We have-- I think it's called
Gitter chat from our GitHub

00:24:11.101 --> 00:24:11.600
page.

00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:13.820
You can find it under the
Google Container tools org.

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:16.546
There's also SIG App,
which we attend regularly,

00:24:16.546 --> 00:24:18.920
which is a Kubernetes special
interest group, which talks

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:20.420
about some of these things.

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:23.780
And the Java and Scaffold
Slacks on Kubernetes--

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:26.600
the Scaffold one definitely
has a lot more people talking

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:29.860
than the Java one, but I'd love
to see more people participate

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:30.920
in that Slack channel.

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:34.250
And I think scaffold also has
a public bi-weekly meeting

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:36.360
that people are welcome
to attend as well.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.030
MELANIE WARRICK: So
if somebody is new,

00:24:38.030 --> 00:24:41.840
like never done anything
with Spring or with Jib

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:44.150
or with Java, and
they are interested,

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:46.550
how would you recommend
them getting involved?

00:24:46.550 --> 00:24:48.560
MIKE ELTSUFIN: If they
have never used Spring,

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:50.210
there are a lot
of sample projects

00:24:50.210 --> 00:24:52.970
we have in our GitHub repo
you can get started with.

00:24:52.970 --> 00:24:54.920
We have pretty
good documentation.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:57.110
And Spring itself has
great documentation.

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:59.420
There are so many books
on Spring and tutorials

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:00.380
you can find.

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:03.560
Besides that, we actually
have code labs, Google Cloud,

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:05.660
that you can follow
along on Google Cloud

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:07.220
and just get things running.

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:08.270
We'll link to that.

00:25:08.270 --> 00:25:10.478
And that's just that you
can experience it right away

00:25:10.478 --> 00:25:13.190
without really knowing too much
about how to build applications

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:14.364
with Spring or GCP.

00:25:14.364 --> 00:25:15.280
MELANIE WARRICK: Cool.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:15.710
Thank you.

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:17.230
MARK MANDEL: Patrick,
anything on your end?

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:19.050
PATRICK FLYNN: The best source
of documentation for Jib

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:20.240
is our GitHub page.

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:20.590
MELANIE WARRICK: Cool.

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:20.940
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:21.300
MELANIE WARRICK: Thank you.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:22.870
MARK MANDEL: Well,
before we wrap up today.

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:24.703
Is there anything that
you want to make sure

00:25:24.703 --> 00:25:26.086
that our listeners know?

00:25:26.086 --> 00:25:27.210
You going be at any events?

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:28.710
Is there anything
we haven't covered

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:30.100
in the podcast, anything at all?

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:30.840
We'd love to hear it.

