00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:18.850
the things.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:19.335
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:19.335 --> 00:26:20.370
It was actually really fun.

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:22.270
I haven't touched Java
in a really long time.

00:26:22.270 --> 00:26:25.190
So it was a really nice chance
to revisit the ecosystem.

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:25.450
MELANIE WARRICK: I know.

00:26:25.450 --> 00:26:27.116
It's been a little
while for me as well.

00:26:27.116 --> 00:26:29.710
So Mark, let me ask you
the question of the week,

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:30.940
as we never do.

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:33.240
What if we have an object
in Google Cloud Storage

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:35.520
and I want to automatically
change an aspect of it?

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:37.650
And some of the examples
that we were mentioning

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.870
is like downgrading the
storage class of objects

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:42.642
older than 365 days to Coldline
storage, which is definitely

00:26:42.642 --> 00:26:44.850
a good question considering
I know a number of people

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:46.850
here about Coldline, but
they don't particularly

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:49.740
think about it; deleting objects
created before a certain date,

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:52.570
like January 1st of
2013 or something;

00:26:52.570 --> 00:26:55.290
and keeping only the
three most recent versions

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:57.445
of each object in a bucket
with versioning enabled.

00:26:57.445 --> 00:26:59.070
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
so this is something

00:26:59.070 --> 00:27:01.570
I actually also came across,
too, for the project I work on.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:04.074
I work on Agones, and we
have development builds.

00:27:04.074 --> 00:27:05.490
We have development
artifacts that

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:08.460
go into Google Cloud Storage
with every PR that comes in.

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:11.560
And obviously, that could grow
to really large proportions.

00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:13.740
So what I wanted to be able
to do is basically say,

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:15.500
if there is a build
that's older than 30 days,

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:16.333
then just delete it.

00:27:16.333 --> 00:27:16.980
We don't care.

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:18.630
I was thinking, oh, maybe
I have to write a script

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:19.838
or do all that kind of stuff.

00:27:19.838 --> 00:27:21.190
I didn't want to do that work.

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:23.100
But within Google
Cloud Storage, there's

00:27:23.100 --> 00:27:25.360
actually a thing called
Object Lifecycle Management,

00:27:25.360 --> 00:27:27.120
which is really, really handy.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:28.599
And you can do this
through gsutil.

00:27:28.599 --> 00:27:30.015
You can do it
through the console.

00:27:30.015 --> 00:27:31.800
You can do it through
REST APIs, too.

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:34.770
But basically you can kind of
just wander into the console,

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:37.410
and grab a bucket, and select
some object conditions-- say,

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:39.630
what age it is maybe,
or the creation date,

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:41.340
or what storage class
it currently is.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:44.670
And then you can say, what is
the action that I want to take?

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:46.170
Do I want to set
this to Nearline?

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:47.503
Do I want to set it to Coldline?

00:27:47.503 --> 00:27:48.630
Maybe I want to delete it.

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:50.590
And I can put these
across my buckets,

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:52.030
which is really nice as well.

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:54.120
And so this means, then,
that it's really easy

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:56.970
to be able to do some pretty
good management of the stuff

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:58.680
that you have in
Google Cloud Storage

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:01.620
and control both your costs, and
also how much stuff you store,

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:04.409
and where it goes without
having to really write any code

00:28:04.409 --> 00:28:05.450
